Chapter: DeskIconGutterTest
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The desk places an icon in the leading gutter of a label, so it has to know
 how wide that gutter RENDERS. Until 2026-08-20 it computed
 `cells * comp-cell-w * ws`, which is the monospace assumption, and the
 defect was invisible to every test in the tree because it only appears once
 a proportional face is actually loaded: with the bitmap fallback the two
 answers agree exactly.

 That is what this arms. The synthetic face below gives every glyph an
 advance of 3, so three spaces measure 9 device pixels. The cell arithmetic
 would answer 48 for the same string at scale 2. The two arms therefore
 DISAGREE by construction, which is the only reason either of them is worth
 printing, and a return to counting characters moves the first arm to 48.

Section: A Face With Known Advances

  dig-gw : Integer = 4

  dig-gh : Integer = 4

  dig-adv : Integer = 3

  dig-adv-off : Integer = 95 * 4 * 4

  dig-fill-adv : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dig-fill-adv (b) (i) =
    if i >= 95 then 0
    else let d = poke-byte b (dig-adv-off + i) dig-adv
    in dig-fill-adv b (i + 1)

  dig-face : Integer -> GopFont
  dig-face (b) =
    GopFont { gf-ok = True, gf-base = b, gf-gw = dig-gw, gf-gh = dig-gh, gf-asc = dig-gh, gf-cap = dig-gh }

Section: Body

  dig-measured : Text
  dig-measured =
    let b = alloc-bytes (95 * dig-gw * dig-gh + 95 + 95)
    in let f = dig-fill-adv b 0
    in let tf = dig-face b
    in let three = desk-text-w tf 2 "   "
    in let two = desk-text-w tf 2 "  "
    in "measured three=" & show three & " two=" & show two

 The no-face arm is the fallback and it is the one that must NOT change: a
 stick with no typeface on its ESP still draws its chrome, and the cell is
 the right answer there because the bitmap face is a fixed cell.

  dig-fallback : Text
  dig-fallback =
    let three = desk-text-w gfont-none 2 "   "
    in let two = desk-text-w gfont-none 2 "  "
    in "fallback three=" & show three & " two=" & show two

 The icon is scaled to the room the gutter actually has, so its drawn width
 can never exceed the gutter it sits in. That is the invariant the overlap
 broke, stated as an arithmetic the test can check rather than as a picture
 somebody looks at: eight is the artwork's width, the scale is the floor of
 the room divided by it, and a gutter under eight pixels still draws at
 scale 1 rather than vanishing.

  dig-scale : Integer -> Integer
  dig-scale (avail) = if avail < 8 then 1 else avail / 8

  dig-fits : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dig-fits (avail) (i) (acc) =
    if i > avail then acc
    else let drawn = 8 * dig-scale i
    in let ok = if i < 8 then 1 else if drawn <= i then 1 else 0
    in dig-fits avail (i + 1) (acc + ok)

  dig-invariant : Text
  dig-invariant =
    let n = dig-fits 200 0 0
    in "icon fits its gutter: " & show n & " of 201"

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni dig-measured
    print-line-uni dig-fallback
    print-line-uni dig-invariant
  end
